= Pulse — Technical =

== Physiological basis ==
The palpable arterial pulse is a pressure wave associated with ventricular ejection. Its perceived amplitude and timing depend on arterial and hemodynamic conditions. Pulse rate approximates beat frequency, but certain arrhythmias may produce a discrepancy between electrical heart rate and effective palpable beats.

== Measurement ==
Pulse counts depend on site, duration, rhythm irregularity and observer or device technique. Optical photoplethysmography measures blood-volume-related light changes, whereas electrocardiography measures electrical activity. Neither method is a direct measurement of [[Cardiac output|cardiac output]] or arterial systolic/diastolic pressure.

== Interpretation ==
Heart rate may vary with body temperature, activity, stress, fitness, medication and illness. A clinically meaningful interpretation requires context and often additional measurements; this article provides no diagnostic thresholds.

